$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Method A: The reaction was carried out in a flat bottomed glass vial
(diameter 15mm) equipped with a magnetic stirrer flea. Alkene (0.212
mmol) was placed in the vial, followed by addition of the complex
((FW=351.42), 223 mg, which corresponds to 0.36-0.39 mmol (1.71-1.86
equivalent) of active oxygen after titration). The mixture was then
dissolved in CH3CN/H2O (95/5) (1 mL) with pyridine (1 eq., 17 •L). The
reaction was stirred at room temperature (reaction times given in the
table 1). The reaction was then dissolved in Et2O (3 mL) and pyridine (2
eq., 34 •L) was added before hydrolysis with H2O (3 mL). The aqueous
phase was extracted with Et2O (4×3 mL). The organic phase was dried over
MgSO4 and after filtration and evaporation, the crude mixture was
analyzed by 1H NMR. Yields and conversions were determined by 1H NMR
relative to an internal standard (para-dimethoxybenzene). The
enantioselectivities were determined by chiral GC using a •-CD column. As
1-methylcyclohexene is volatile, the reaction was performed in deuterated
solvent. The reaction was diluted in CDCl3 (2 mL) in the presence of
internal standard and pyridine (2 eq., 34 •L) before hydrolysis with H2O
(3 mL). The organic phase was separated from the aqueous phase and dried
over MgSO4 before performing analysis. (S,S)-1-phenylcyclohexene
oxide[4], yield 71% 1H NMR (400 MHz, CDCl3, 25 °C): = 1.20-1.71 (m, 4H;
CH2), 1.92-2.38 (m, 4H; CH2), 3.01 (m, 1H; CHO), 7.21-7.53 (m, 5H;
aromatic H). The absolute configuration was determined by comparing the
measured optical rotation with the reported one: [α]D20 = -54.8 (c = 0.47
in toluene) [lit.[5] for the (R,R)-1-phenylcyclohex-1-ene oxide: [α]D25 =
+116.7 (c = 1.21 in benzene) for 98% ee].
