$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General/Typical Procedure: General Procedure for the Epoxidation of the
Enecarbamates 1 with Dimethyldioxirane (GP1) The enecarbamate 1 (1.0
equiv.) was dissolved in acetone (30 mL) and 0.50 g of molecular sieves
(5 Å) was added under an argon-gas atmosphere. The solution was cooled to
0 °C and after 10 min magnetic stirring, 1.0 equiv. of dimethyldioxirane
(0.09 M in acetone) was added. The reaction mixture was allowed to warm
up to ca. 20 °C while stirring, and the reaction progress was monitored
for the presence of DMD by iodometry (KI/HOAC). After complete DMD
consumption (the reaction times are given for the individual cases), the
reaction mixture was centrifuged to remove the molecular sieves, the
sieves were washed with acetone (2x15 mL) and centrifuged again. From the
combined centrifugates, the acetone was removed (20 °C, 30 mbar) and the
epoxide 2 was obtained as only product. The epoxides 2 were too labile
for purification by silica-gel chromatography, and were characterized by
their 1H-and 13C-NMR spectra and exact mass determination on the
diastereomeric mixture [except epoxide 2a, which decomposed completely
already at room temperature (ca. 20 °C) in a couple of hours]. The
diastereomeric ratio was determined by 1H-NMR analysis directly on the
crude reaction mixture, through integration of the areas under the
characteristic NMR signals. In the case of nearly 1:1 mixtures of the
diastereomers, COSY, HMBC and HMQC experiments were carried out for
rigorous assignment. For the configurational assignment of the individual
diastereomers, see General Procedure GP3. The epoxides 2b-d persist as
neat solids for several months at -20 °C, but they readily decompose in
solution in the presence of acid traces at room temperature (ca. 20 °C).
For this reason, after formation, the labile epoxides must be immediately
worked up to remove the solvent and the molecular sieves to minimize
decomposition. Epoxidation of the Enecarbamate Z-1a by DMD According to
the general procedure GP1, 397.5 mg (1.96 mmol) of enecarbamate Z-1a were
epoxidized with DMD during 1 h. After work-up, the racemic epoxide
(1S*,2S*)-2a was obtained as colorless oil in 94% yield (403.8 mg). The
material decomposed at ca. 20 °C within a couple of hours, such that an
exact-mass determination was not possible. Characterisation rests only on
the NMR-spectral data. Epoxidation of the Enecarbamate Z-1c by DMD
According to the general procedure GP1, 619.8 mg (2.22 mmol) of the
enecarbamate Z-1c were epoxidized with DMD for 4 h. After work-up, a
diastereomeric mixture (92:8) of the epoxides (1R,2R)-2c and (1S,2S)-2c
was obtained as colorless oil in ca. 100% yield (655.1 mg).
(1R,2R)-4-Phenyl-3-(3-methyl-3-phenyloxyranyl)oxazolidin-2-one
[(1R,2R)-2c].
(1S,2S)-4-Phenyl-3-(3-methyl-3-phenyloxyranyl)oxazolidin-2-one
[(1S,2S)-2c]. IR (neat) 2974, 1763, 1456, 1396, 1320, 1224; exact mass
for C18H17NO3 Calcd. 295.1208; Found 295.1205. 1H-NMR (400 MHz, CDCl3) δ
1.46 (s, 3 H), 3.78-3.85 (m, 1 H), 3.87-3.95 (m, 1 H), 4.19-4.27 (m, 1
H), 4.52 (s, 1 H), 6.76-6.82 (m, 2 H), 7.11-7.25 (m, 8 H); 13C-NMR (151
MHz, CDCl3) δ 22.9 (q), 58.4 (d), 62.2 (s), 69.1 (t), 70.7 (d), 125.9
(d), 126.8 (d), 127.9 (d), 128.0 (d), 128.6 (d), 128.9 (d), 136.7 (s),
138.4 (s), 158.6 (s). 1H-NMR (400 MHz, CDCl3) δ 1.52 (s, 3 H), 3.77-4.16
(m, 3 H, overlap with the signals of the major isomer), 4.45 (s, 1 H),
6.76-6.82 (m, 5 H), 7.11-7.25 (m, 5 H); 13C-NMR (151 MHz, CDCl3): δ =
22.5 (q), 59.1 (d), 65.1 (s), 68.1 (t), 70.6 (d), 126.1 (d), 126.5 (d),
127.9 (d), 128.2 (d), 128.5 (d), 129.1 (d), 136.8 (s), 139.0 (s), 159.1
(s).
